---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Getting Your First Business License — LicensingMadeSimple" description="A plain-English guide to getting your first business license.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Getting Your First Business License</h1>
      <p>What you need, where to get it, and how long it takes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container article" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>Do You Need One?</h2>
      <p>Almost certainly yes. Most cities and counties require a general business license to operate any type of business. Some industries require additional state-level licenses on top of that. Operating without required licenses can result in fines, forced closure, and personal liability.</p>

      <h2>What Kind Do You Need?</h2>
      <p>Start with your local city or county. They'll tell you whether you need a general business license (most businesses do). Then check your state's requirements for your specific industry. Regulated industries like financial services, healthcare, construction, and food service almost always require state-level licenses.</p>
      <p>Not sure what applies to you? Use our <a href="/#wizard">roadmap wizard</a> for a quick assessment.</p>

      <h2>What It Takes</h2>
      <p>The requirements vary, but most business licenses involve filling out an application form with your business details, paying a government filing fee (ranges from $25 to several thousand dollars depending on the license type), providing proof of business formation (articles of incorporation, LLC documents, etc.), passing a background check (for regulated industries), and posting a surety bond (for some license types).</p>
      <p>The complexity ranges from "fill out one form and pay $50" for a simple city business license to "prepare a 50-page application package with financial statements, business plans, and $500K in bonds" for something like a money transmitter license.</p>

      <h2>How Long It Takes</h2>
      <p>Simple business licenses: 1-4 weeks. Trade licenses (contractor, real estate): 2-8 weeks. Financial services licenses: 1-12 months. The biggest variable isn't the state's processing time. It's how long it takes to prepare a clean application. Incomplete or incorrect applications get sent back, adding weeks of delay each time.</p>

      <h2>The DIY vs. Professional Choice</h2>
      <p>You can do this yourself. But here's the tradeoff: professionals who've done hundreds of thousands of filings have a 97% first-submission approval rate. DIY applicants see significantly more rejection and correction cycles, each adding 2-4 weeks of delay.</p>
      <p>The math usually works like this: your time spent researching requirements, finding forms, filling out applications, and handling corrections is worth more than the professional service fee. That's especially true for multi-state licensing, where you'd multiply the research and paperwork across every state.</p>

      <p>Ready to get started? <a href="/#wizard">Get your personalized roadmap</a> in 30 seconds.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .article h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; margin-top: 32px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .article h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .article p { font-size: 0.93rem; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 14px; }
</style>
